Detailed Baseline Failure Analysis
Parallel Sequential Scans: Because no indexes exist on TRANSACTIONS.initiated_timestamp or foreign keys, the database engine scans all 8.5 million rows from physical storage into memory buffers.
Hash Join Memory Overhead: The engine reads the entire ACCOUNTS table to build an in-memory hash table, spilling to temporary disk space (work_mem exhaustion) if allocated memory limits are exceeded.
Expensive External Sort: Sorting thousands of unindexed transaction records by timestamp requires an external disk merge-sort, driving up query latency to 1,850ms–2,400ms, which breaches sub-second SLA thresholds.
3.3 Indexing Strategy & Architectural Optimisation
To eliminate full table scans and reduce memory usage during joins and sorting, we apply a multi-tier indexing strategy:
Code
+------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------+
| Target Table     | Index Type                  | Indexed Columns       | Operational Purpose                   |
+------------------+-----------------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------+
| ACCOUNTS         | Unique B-Tree Clustered     | account_id            | Instant PK point lookups              |
| ACCOUNTS         | Non-Clustered B-Tree        | customer_id           | Speeds up customer-to-account joins   |
| TRANSACTIONS     | Composite B-Tree            | (source_account_id,   | Covers directional history searches   |
|                  |                             |  initiated_timestamp) | without sorting                       |
| TRANSACTIONS     | Composite B-Tree            | (dest_account_id,     | Covers incoming transfer searches     |
|                  |                             |  initiated_timestamp) | without sorting                       |
| TRANSACTIONS     | Partial/Filtered B-Tree     | transaction_id, amount| Indexes only unsettled items          |
|                  |                             | WHERE status='Pending'| for rapid clearing queue processing   |
